# Information and its atom, the bit and its exclusive-or. The smallest unit of information is one bit, and the operation
# that combines two bits without losing information is the exclusive-or, the parity of their sum. Under exclusive-or the
# bits form the group of order two: it has an identity, the zero bit, every bit is its own inverse, and it is commutative
# and associative. This is the structure underneath every linear error-correcting code, every parity check and syndrome,
# and the classical channel through which entangled parties share information. This file DEFINES the bit and its
# exclusive-or and PROVES the group laws that make information composable: zero is the identity, every bit is its own
# inverse, and the operation is commutative and associative. It records the Bell-pair mutual information as a machine-
# integer fact. The group laws are quantified over the actual bit type and checked by induction.

# The two values of a bit.
form bit
  case off
  case on

# Bit negation, the flip used by exclusive-or.
task flip
  take a, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        make on
    case on
      send back
        make off

# Exclusive-or: zero leaves the other bit unchanged, one flips it. The parity of the two bits, the information-preserving
# combination.
task xor
  take a, like bit
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read a
    case off
      send back
        read b
    case on
      send back
        call flip
          read b

# ZERO IS THE IDENTITY: combining any bit with the zero bit leaves it unchanged, xor(off, a) = a, for every a. Proved by
# induction over the bit.
rule zero-is-the-identity
  seat a, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call xor
        make off
        read a
      read a
  fold a

# EVERY BIT IS ITS OWN INVERSE: a bit combined with itself gives zero, xor(a, a) = off, for every a. So the group has
# exponent two, the defining property of parity. Proved by induction over the bit.
rule every-bit-is-its-own-inverse
  seat a, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call xor
        read a
        read a
      make off
  fold a

# EXCLUSIVE-OR IS COMMUTATIVE: the order of the two bits does not matter, xor(a, b) = xor(b, a), for all a and b. Proved
# by simultaneous induction over the two bits.
rule exclusive-or-is-commutative
  seat a, like bit
  seat b, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call xor
        read a
        read b
      call xor
        read b
        read a
  fold a, b

# EXCLUSIVE-OR IS ASSOCIATIVE: regrouping does not matter, xor(xor(a, b), c) = xor(a, xor(b, c)), for all a, b and c, so
# a string of bits has a well-defined parity. Proved by simultaneous induction over the three bits.
rule exclusive-or-is-associative
  seat a, like bit
  seat b, like bit
  seat c, like bit
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call xor
        call xor
          read a
          read b
        read c
      call xor
        read a
        call xor
          read b
          read c
  fold a, b, c

# THE BELL-PAIR MUTUAL INFORMATION is two: each qubit alone has entropy one, the pair together entropy zero, so the shared
# information is one plus one minus zero, 2 = (1 + 1) - 0, the maximum for one qubit on each side.
hold bell-pair-mutual-information-is-two
  call is-equal
    code 2
    call subtract
      call add
        code 1
        code 1
      code 0
